The transport manager controls how SymmetricDS nodes communicate with each other during synchronization.
By default, SymmetricDS uses a hybrid transport that automatically selects the most efficient communication
method based on whether the target node is running in the same JVM or on a remote server.

The transport type is configured using the `transport.type` parameter in the engine properties file or in
the <<PARAMETER>> table.

[source, properties]
----
transport.type=hybrid
----

==== Transport Types

The following values are supported for the `transport.type` parameter:

hybrid:: The default transport type. The hybrid transport manager automatically routes communication between
two underlying transports: an internal in-JVM transport and an HTTP transport. When the target node is running
in the same JVM (for example, multiple engine instances in a single SymmetricDS server), it uses the internal
transport for direct in-process communication, which avoids the overhead of HTTP serialization and network calls.
When the target node is on a remote server, it falls back to HTTP transport. This makes hybrid the best choice
for most deployments because it provides optimal performance without requiring any additional configuration.

http:: Uses HTTP (or HTTPS) for all node-to-node communication, regardless of whether the nodes are co-located
in the same JVM. This is useful when you want to guarantee that all communication goes through the HTTP layer,
such as when testing HTTP-specific behavior or when a proxy, load balancer, or firewall rule depends on seeing
all traffic as HTTP requests. In most cases, `hybrid` is preferred because it offers the same HTTP behavior
for remote nodes while optimizing communication for co-located nodes.

internal:: Uses only the internal in-JVM transport. This transport type is intended for scenarios where all
communicating nodes are running within the same JVM. It bypasses HTTP entirely, passing data directly between
engine instances in memory. If a target node is not in the same JVM, communication will not succeed.

file:: Uses file-based transport. Instead of communicating directly between nodes, data is written to and
read from the local file system. This transport type is used in conjunction with <<Offline Synchronization>>
where batch files are manually moved between nodes.

==== Choosing a Transport Type

For most deployments, the default `hybrid` transport is recommended. It handles both co-located and remote
node communication transparently without any special configuration.

Consider using a different transport type in the following situations:

* Use `http` when all communication must pass through the HTTP layer, for example, when traffic monitoring
or proxy infrastructure requires it.
* Use `internal` when all nodes are guaranteed to run in the same JVM and you want to avoid any HTTP overhead.
* Use `file` when nodes are disconnected and batch files must be transported manually or through an external
process.

==== Custom HTTP Transport

The HTTP transport manager implementation can be overridden by setting the `http.transport.manager.class`
parameter to the fully qualified class name of a custom implementation. The custom class must extend
`HttpTransportManager` and provide a constructor that accepts an `ISymmetricEngine` parameter.

[source, properties]
----
http.transport.manager.class=com.example.MyHttpTransportManager
----

This applies to both the `http` and `hybrid` transport types, since the hybrid transport uses an HTTP
transport manager internally for remote communication.
